Duranium USB debug layer (duranium-usb-debug)
=============================================

What it is
----------
A development-only debug layer for postmarketOS Duranium, modelled on the
SM-T570 mainline bring-up console. It brings up a USB NCM network interface
and, over that interface only:

  * ssh on TCP 22           -> a dedicated sshd, keys only (no passwords)
  * pairing on TCP 2324     -> a computer sends its ssh public key; the
                               tablet asks "Allow USB debugging?" with the
                               key's fingerprint and adds it only on "Allow"

and, only in open mode (below):

  * root shell on TCP 2323    -> busybox "telnetd -l /bin/sh" (no auth)
  * register console on TCP 23 -> gta3-console from the device package
  * kernel log on TCP 24       -> "dmesg -w"
  * adb (duranium-usb-debug-adb) -> adbd on FunctionFS (no auth)

Fixed addressing (the host tools in the device repository rely on it):

  * gadget VID:PID 0x1d6b:0x0104
  * device MAC 02:57:0a:00:00:01  -> device link-local  <USB link-local address>
  * host   MAC 02:57:0a:00:00:02  -> host link-local     <USB link-local address>

All sockets are socket-activated (Accept=yes) on the wildcard address but
bound to the gadget interface with SO_BINDTODEVICE (BindToDevice=usb0),
which works before the link has carrier and before IPv6 DAD finished.

Pairing a computer
------------------
From the device repository: scripts/tab-ssh --pair (prints this computer's
key fingerprint; tap Allow on the tablet if it shows the same one), then
scripts/tab-ssh [command]. By hand:

  ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_gta3_usb
  nc ${GTA3_CONSOLE_ADDR} 2324 < ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_gta3_usb.pub
  ssh -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_gta3_usb root@${GTA3_CONSOLE_ADDR}

Paired keys live in /etc/duranium-usb-debug/authorized_keys (root only).
"duranium-usb-debug keys" lists them, "duranium-usb-debug unpair N" forgets
one. The prompt goes to the active graphical session on seat0; with nobody
logged in, or no answer in 60 s, the request is denied.

On the screen, on and off
-------------------------
While the debug gadget holds the USB port, a notification "USB debugging is
on" appears once per connection (not again while the cable stays in; again
after it was out for 10 s), with a "Turn off" button. Under Do Not Disturb
it goes to the notification list only.

  duranium-usb-debug status     on/off, open mode, USB state, paired count
  duranium-usb-debug off        off until "on": no debug network, no ssh,
                                no pairing; the USB port goes back to the
                                system's USB modes. Lasts across reboots
                                and updates (/etc/duranium-usb-debug/disabled).
                                The person at the screen may do this
                                without a password (polkit rule).
  sudo duranium-usb-debug on

duranium.usb_debug=0 on the kernel command line is the hard off that also
covers the initrd.

While the debug gadget holds the port, Nura's USB mode chooser
(usb-moded-notify) is not started: usb-signaller sees the gadget as the
read-only mode "Netboot", so every choice failed and the chooser came back
again and again. After "off" the chooser works as usual.

Open mode
---------
duranium.usb_debug=open on the kernel command line turns on the
unauthenticated root shell, register console, kernel log stream and adbd,
in the initrd and the booted system. Bring-up images set it. On a dev image
it is typed once in the boot menu (systemd-boot "e"): that editor already
gives anyone at the screen root, so open mode adds no new way in. The
indicator says "Open mode" while it is on.

Without open mode the initrd brings up only the network interface: the disk
passphrase is typed on the screen (scripts/tab-unlock needs open mode).

SECURITY
--------
Default (dev images): no unauthenticated access. ssh accepts only keys that
were installed by root or allowed on the screen; nothing listens on Wi-Fi or
any routable link (BindToDevice=usb0, and the firewall rule
60_duranium_usb_debug.nft opens the ports on usb* only). Open mode is an
unauthenticated root shell for anyone on the cable: bring-up only.

Release images
--------------
This layer is a separate package. Release images are built without it.
